rem$ Orton : Key to the species Myxacium and Phlegmacium

1,2 $ Cap and stem viscid, at least at first; flesh soon soft - $$s" I. Myxacium"
1,21 $ Cap viscid at least at first, stem dry; flesh firm or soft $$s" II Phlegmacium"

I. MYXACIUM
2,3 $ Taste immediately bitter, at least in cap cuticle; spores less than long, punctate to almostsmooth, if subglobose, smaller, 4½-6/3½-4½ µm 
2,9 $ Taste mild (rarely with sl. bitter after taste) ; spores rough to very rough, mostly over10 µm  long, or subglobose, 6½ -10/6-8 µm 
3,0 $ Cap pale to deep violaceous at first, soon discolouring pale ochraceous; (stem ventricose-fusiform ± pointed at base; under beech) $ croceo-caeruleus (14)
3,4 $ Cap never violaceous
4,0 $ Cap bright orange-yellow or golden-tawny with paler non-striate margin; cap cuticle and flesh bitter; stem white, ventricose-fusiform or clavate, ± pointed at base; spores elliptic- to ovate-pruniform, 6½-8/4½-5½ µm  $ vibratilis (15)
4,5 $ Cap paler in colour, white to ochraceous or buff, or if tinged tawny or orange, cap margin striate or cap cuticle only bitter or stem coloured slightly or spores different shape
5,0 $ Flesh mild, cap cuticle bitter; stem firm; flesh and gills becoming rather deep coloured (yellowish or ochraceous-buff)$causticus (16)
5,6 $ Both flesh and cap cuticle bitter; stem often soon soft (except ochroleucus (20)); flesh on the whole paler
6,0 $ Cap and stem nearly always dry; stem firm and tough; cap with white silky sheen; (spores elliptic-pruniform, 7-8/4-5 µm ; esp. under oaks) $ ochroleucus (20)
6,7 $ Cap and stem viscid at least when young; stem soon ± soft; cap with or without white silky sheen
7,0 $ Cap small, 12-30(40) mm., disc often tinged tawny, margin striate when moist; gills rather broad and distant, very pale; stem equal or sl. swollen below, often flexuose, very soft and slender, often remaining viscid; (spores elliptic or sl. amygdaliform, 6½ -8/4- 5 µm ) $ pluvius (17)
7,8 $ Cap medium to large, rarely small, (15)30-100 mm., paler, margin never striate; gills often narrower and more crowded or darker; stem equal to fusiform with ± pointed base, often firmer at first and more robust, soon dry
8,0 $ Spores subglobose to broadly ovate, 4½-6/3½-4½ µm ; stem often fusiform or attenuated downwards; gills often narrowly adnate and narrow $ cristallinus (19)
8,0 $ Spores elliptic-amygdaliform, 7-9/4-5 µm ; stem equal or sl. swollen near ± pointed base; gills often emarginato-adnate and ± ventricose $ emollitus (18)
9,10 $ Spores subglobose or broadly ovate, 6½-io/6-8 µm ; viscid veil on stem smooth making the stem look varnished when dry
9,13 $ Spores ± amygdaliform to limoniform, more than 10 µm  long (rarely less than 10 µm  and then elliptic); viscid veil on stem often floccose .
10,0 $ Cap bright blue or violaceous at first; (stem ventricose or clavate-acuminate, blue at first $ salor (12)
10,11 $ Cap without distinct blue or violaceous tints at first (sometimes with sl. violaceous flush in epipoleus (13))
11,0 $ Gills deep blue or blue violaceous at first; common species; (cap ± yellow sometimes tinged tawny; stem ± clavate, white, often smeared with yellowish veil below cortinal zone) $ delibutus (10)
11,12 $ Gills not blue or violaceous at first; uncommon species
12,0 $ Cap pale- to golden-yellow; gills pinkish- or ochraceous-clay, then buff; stem clavate  or swollen at base, white, or tinged ochraceous below; (resembling a slender delibutus,  but without blue tints) $ illibatus (11)
12,0 $ Cap ochraceous-grey with olive-violaceous flush then dingy white with livid ochre  centre; gills clay at first; stem clavate or fusiform, ± pointed at base $ epipoleus (13)
13,14 $ Gill edge with conspicuous balloon-shaped, pyriform or clavate cystidia, 10-30 µm  wide;  cap with markedly striate or wrinkled margin or smooth; spores amygdaliform to limoniform
13,17 $ Gill edge without such conspicuous cystidia, but sterile 4- clavate cells about 8-12 µm   wide resembling basidia may be present; cap with smooth or more rarely sl. striate or  grooved margin; spores elliptic to limoniform
14,0 $ Gills violaceous at first, then violaceous rusty or purplish-umber, always rather dark  coloured, often interveined and veined on the sides; cap remaining conical or conicoexp.  for a long time, margin strongly wrinkled-striate; stem often rather long and robust,  ± ventricose $ elatior (6)
14,15 $ Gills never violaceous, paler in colour, ochraceous-clay to rusty- buff, rarely interveined  but sometimes veined on the sides; cap often expanding, if ±- conical margin smooth or  sl. wrinkled-striate or colours paler; stem slender or robust, equal or ventricose 
15,0$ Cap pale to deep yellowish or ochraceous, often with tawny tinge at disc, margin smooth  or almost so; stem rather long, almost pure white, sometimes with sl. violaceous tinge$pseudosalor (8)
15,16 $ Cap never so brightly coloured, at least disc not ochraceous but livid or olivaceous,  margin smooth or not; stem short or long, ± violaceous or bluish at least in part
16,0 $ Stem rather slender and soft; cap often small, 18-50 mm., at first covered with blue-violaceous  gluten then greyish- or livid-brown with smooth margin; gills subdistant, ± ventricose $ stillatitius (9)
16,0 $ Stem often stouter and firmer;cap generally larger, 30-110 mm., livid- or olive-brown,  never violaceous, margin often striate or grooved; gills f. crowded, not or sl. ventricose $ mucifluus (7)
17,18 $ Stem with conspicuous whitish or pale bluish scales on a darker (tawny, chestnut or baybrown)  background
17,19 $ Stem without pale scales on a darker background, white, violaceous or tinged ochraceous
18,0 $ Cap bright coloured, ± tawny; spores 12-15/7-8 µm  or larger; stem apex and gills sometimes  bluish at first; typically under conifers, also under beech $ collinitus (1)
18,0 $ Cap duller coloured, clay- or bay-brown but sometimes with tawny tinge at margin;  spores 10-13/6-7 µm ; no blue tints anywhere; under deciduous trees, generally in wet places $ trivialis (2)
19,0 $ Cap becoming greyish with silky sheen at least in part; stem violaceous in part; spores  elliptic, 7½ -9/4½-5 µm  $ livido-ochraceus (4)
19,20 $ Cap never greyish with silky sheen; stem not violaceous; spores elliptic-amygdaliform to  sublimoniform, 10½-16/5½-7 µm 
20,0 $ Cap ± chestnut, then yellow-brown esp. at margin; gills not  violaceous at first; stem equal, white; spores elongate sublimoniform, 13-16/6-7 µm . $ mucosus (3)
20,0 $ Cap egg- or orange-yellow; gills pale violaceous at first; stem ± clavate, whitish sometimes  tinged yellowish in part; spores elliptic-amygdaliform, 10½-11/5½-6 ¾  µm . $ Metrodi (5)

II. PHLEGMACIUM.

21,22 $ Gills and flesh discolouring ± deep violet or purplish when bruised or rubbed; (spores 8-11/5-6 µm )
21,24 $ Not so (not : Gills and flesh discolouring ± deep violet or purplish when bruised or rubbed )
22,0 $ Gills at first pale to pallid-buff (rarely with sl. lilac tinge); cap yellow-buff to pallid  date-brown, not violet $ subpurpurascens (54)
22,23 $ Gills at first deep violet; cap pale yellowish to dark umber, sometimes ± violaceous esp.  near margin
23,0 $ Cap tawny-buff to dark umber, margin often deep violet at first; stem  with varia $ purpurascens (53)
23,0 $ Cap pale yellowish to pale date-brown, sometimes with sl. violaceous tinge, margin  never deep violet; stem less robust, equal or sl. clavate; small to medium, rarely larger $ porphyropus (55)
24,25 $ Stem with well-marked wide marginate (Fig. 1) or rounded marginate (Fig. 6) bulb,  more rarely variable and then sometimes immarginately bulbous $$s"   Scauri"
24,84 $ Stem not marginately bulbous, equal, clavate or irregularly bulbous $$s"   Cliduchi-elastici"


A. SCAURI. (See also § 22)
25,26 $ Young gills white, whitish or clay $$s"   Leucophylli"
25,41 $ Young gills lilac, bluish or violaceous $$s"   Cyanophylli"
25,66 $ Young gills yellow to olive or greenish $$s"   Xanthophylli" 


a. LEUCOPHYLLI
26,0 $ Smell strong and persistent, fragrant, of orange blossom; (cap yellowish-buff with  yellower margin; spores amygdaliform, 9-10/5-6 µm ) $ evosmus (30)
26,27 $ If smell strong, not of orange blossom
27,28 $ Cap at first white, whitish, clay or pale creamy-ochre
27,32 $ Cap at first pale yellow to chestnut
28,29 $ Stem with conspicuous wide marginate bulb; flesh sometimes with bluish tinge
28,30 $ Stem with ± rounded or narrow free-edged marginate bulb, rarely almost immarginate; flesh never  bluish
29,0 $ Spores amygdaliform to sublimoniform, 10-12/6-6½ µm -,a; cap white at first discolouring to ochraccous-  buff; flesh of stem apex with fugacious bluish tinge; generally robust $ caroviolaceus (34)
29,0 $ Spores elliptic-pruniform, 7¼ -9/4-5 µm ; cap whitish or pale clay to pale buff; flesh not bluish at stem  apex; generally less robust $ rapaceus (33)
30,0 $ Spores elliptic-amygdaliform, 10-11/5½-6 µm  cap almost unicolorous pale creamy-ochre;  (stem with f. conspicuous but rounded marginate bulb; taste mild) * $ ochropallidus (23)
30,31 $ Spores elliptic-amygdaliform to sublimoniform, less than 10 µm , long; cap pale clay to ochraceous or  buff, disc often darker
31,0 $ Cap pale clay to pale ochraceous or buff; cap cuticle often bitter; stem bulb narrow marginate to  rounded marginate, often inconspicuous; spores elliptic-amygdaliform, 8-10/4½-5½ µm  $ amarescens (25)
31,0 $ Cap creamy-ochre, often tinged tawny-ochre at disc; taste mild; stem bulb f. conspicuous, rounded  marginate; spores amygdaliform to sublimoniform, 6-7½/4-4½ µm ; (cap 30-50 mm.; stem 40-60/5-8  mm. (8-12 mm. in bulb); mixed woods) . * ( ($minus Vel.)
32,0 $ Cap unicolorous tawny-reddish at first, then paler at disc; spores ±elliptic 8½-10/4½-5½ µm ; (stem  with conspicuous ± rounded marginate bulb, becoming tinged with colour of cap) $ allutus (24)
32,33 $ Cap from yellow to chestnut at first, if with a tendency to be tawny-reddish spores more than 10 µm   long or differently shaped
33,0 $ Cap at first pale primrose or chrome-yellow, then deeper yellow with disc generally tinged ochraceous;  gills for a long time white; stem with ± conspicuous wide marginate bulb; flesh soon tinged ochraceousyellow,  very firm at first; spores elliptic-amygdaliform 8-10(11)/5-6 µm  $ Langei (29)
33,34 $ If cap pure yellow at first characters otherwise
34,35 $ Stem with variable sharply marginate, rounded marginate or almost immarginate never wide marginate  bulb; cap yellow-ochraceous or buff generally with yellower margin; flesh soon soft and ± yellowish
34,36 $ Stem with wide marginate bulb, or if less marked, then cap with marked orange tints, or stem with  violaceous veil when young; if cap yellowish or ochraceous, flesh firmer and often white
35,0 $ Spores ± limoniform, 10-11/5½-6½ µm ; smell often strong, of apples or acid $ multiformis (21)
35,0 $ Spores elliptic to elliptic-amygdaliform, 8-9/4½-5 µm ; smell faint or strong of honey $ melliolens (22)
36,0 $ Spores subglobose to ovato-pruniform, 8-12/6½-8 µm ; cap ochraceous or rusty, margin often with olivaceous  tinge $ turbinatus (31)
36,37 $ Spores amygdaliform to limoniform, rarely elliptic fusiform, sizes various; cap rarely with olivaceous  tints
37,0 $ Spores broadly amygdaliform, punctate-rough, 7-8/4-5 µm ; (cap 40-70 mm., bright orange-yellow to orange-  brown, sometimes with yellower margin; stem 30-60/13-25 mm. (20-30 mm. in bulb), with distinct  or almost immarginate bulb; flesh pale yellowish-white, very hard; in fir and mixed woods) *($aurantiacus Moser)
37,0 $ Spores elliptic fusiform, almost smooth, 7½ -9/3 ½-4 µm ; (cap pale ochraceous to brick-red, innately fibrillose;  stem white, discolouring when touched; flesh white; under conifers)  * ($roseo-limbatus Secr, var marginatus Bres.)
37,38 $ Spores more than 9 µm , long, ± rough; (stem bulb generally well marked)
38,0 $ Stem with remains of violaceous veil at base at least when young, sometimes sl. bluish or glaucous  at apex; (cap yellowish-ochraceous or buff with innate reddish-brown fibrils, margin sometimes  reddish- or tawny-brown; under pines) * $ pinetorum (32)
38,39 $ Stem never with violaceous veil or bluish tints
39,0 $ Cap chestnut or date-brown-tawny to smoky-brown, very viscid, smooth; spores amygdaliform  to ± limoniform, 12-15/7-9 µm ; in coniferous woods $ napus (26)
39,40 $ Cap paler, either soon dry and flocculose, or less viscid and with scattered  whitish patches of veil at first; spores smaller
40,0 $ Cap rusty or pallid clay-brown often with darker spots, soon dry, opaque and flocculose; spores  sublimoniform to almost fusiform, 9-12/4-5(6) µm ; mountainous conifer woods $ corrosus (27)
40,0 $ Cap ochraceous-, tawny- or rusty-buff, at first with whitish patches of veil and yellowish margin;  spores ± limoniform, 10-14/6-7½ µm  beech woods, often on chalk . $ subturbinatus (28)(b)

