rem$ Key 2. Alternative key to section Anomali(including two other species which might be thought to belong to this section).

1,0 $ Lower part of stem and sometimes also cap with distinct reddish or reddish-brown  scales on a paler background $ spilomeus (129)
1,2 $ Not so
2,0 $ Gills rather persistently amethyst-blue then clay-bluish not turning purplish; cap  fuliginous tinged with lilac, becoming white-silky-noary, not turning reddish;  stem without distinct second veil; (spores elliptic-oblong, 10 µm  long sec Quélet $ myrtillinus (130)
2,3 $ If gills rather persistently bluish, cap becoming tinged reddish, tawny-buff or umber  or distinct second veil present
3,0 $ Spores broadly elliptic, 7-8½/5-6 µm , Fig. 8); smell ± radishy at least when cut; stem with lilac  or blue-violaceous peronate veil in addition to cortina; (gills becoming dark violaceousdate-  brown or umber) $ simulatus (110)
3,4 $ Spores subglobose or broadly ovate, 6½-9/5-7 µm , (Figs. 6,7) or 8-11/6-8 µm , (Figs. 9, 10);  smell not radishy; second veil, if present, yellowish or pallid.
4,0 $ Entirely without blue-violaceous tints even when young (except very occ. a vague  bluish tinge at stem-apex); cap and stem pallid-whitish or pale buff then pallid- or  yellowish-ochraceous; (gills whitish or clay at first; stem with sparse second veil;  spores 7-9 (9½)/5½- 6½ (7) µm  (Fig. 7) $ tabularis (128)
4,5 $ At least gills blue- or grey-violaceous at first, often also stem-apex; cap dirty  ochraceous or pallid-grey to ochraceous-buff, date-brown or umber, sometimes  changing to tawny-buff or rusty
5,0 $ Spores 6½-8½/5-7 µm ; gills, stem-apex and sometimes cap margin blue-violaceous at first;  stem with adpressed sometimes fugacious yellowish or dirty patches of veil below  cortinal zone, sl. clavate, 4-12 mm. in diam.; cap soon reddish-brown, date-brown or  umber, generally becoming expanded ± orbicular, less commonly umbonate often thin fleshed $ lepidopus (127)
5,6 $ Spores 8-11/6-8 µm ; from entirely blue-violaceous to blue-violaceous only in young gills;  stem with or without second veil; cap colours various, often broadly umbonate and ±  thick-fleshed at least at disc
6,7 $ Cap soon bright tawny-buff or rusty-tawny to rusty-brown, clay-brownish or ochraceous-  buff to date-brownish at first (sometimes tinged sl. blue-violaceous in $ caninus)
6,8 $ Cap blue-violaceous, pallid-grey, ochraceous-buff or brownish, if becoming rusty or  tawny-tinged only so when quite old
7,0 $ Stem clavate, often robust (10-20 mm. in diam.) with pallid or dirty-brownish veil forming  ± distinct ring-zone below cortinal zone; flesh blue-violaceous in stem-apex at first; in  woods, especially under conifers $ caninus (124)
7,0 $ Stem ± equal or clavate, generally slender (3-10 mm. in diam., up to 18 mm. at base),  without second veil, white silky striate below cortinal zone; flesh and stem white at first;  in grass, especially on basic soils, probably also in woods $ epsomiensis (125)
8,0 $ Habit generally robust; stem usually distinctly clavate-bulbous, 10-30 mm. at base;  smell fairly strong when cut, sickly sweet; (cap ochraceous or yellowish-buff with  or without bluish or paler margin, sometimes becoming wrinkled or rugulose; in  woods especially birch, probably also conifers) $ azureovelatus (123)
8,9 $ Habit relatively slender; stem clavate or not, 5-15 mm. at base; smell none or faintly  sickly-sweet .
9,0 $ Almost entirely blue-violaceous at first; gills rather persistently blue-violaceous, finally  violaceous-umber; (cap and stem discolouring pallid or yellowish with age, disc of cap  sometimes tinged tawny when old) $ azureus (126)
9,10 $ Blue-violaceous colours restricted to gills, stem-apex and flesh when young; gills becoming  clay-buff or milky-coffee then rusty-buff.
10,0 $ Stem with distinct yellowish second veil; cap pallid-grey or ashy-sepia discolouring  dirty ochraceous, olive-grey or pallid-buff, finally pallid-tan or rusty-buff, grey silky  at first with strong silky-atomate sheen; in woods $ anomalus (122)
10,0 $ Stem without second veil, smooth below cortinal zone; cap brownish then rusty-buff  with white silky sheen, disc often becoming wrinkled; in beech woods  *(C. $ diabolicus Fr.)


